# Member completion never offers Openplanet-injected methods (MwAddRef, GetCurrentEntityID, GetManialinkPages)

## Steps to Reproduce

1. TODO: Add steps

## Expected Behavior

TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior


Found 2026-08-30 while landing B059. Not a regression — this gap predates
B059 and is symmetric across every injected method.

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Open a plugin with the type DB loaded.
2. `void f(CSmPlayer@ player) { player. }` — trigger member completion.
3. `GetCurrentEntityID` is not offered. Same for `MwAddRef` / `MwRelease` on
   any `CMwNod@` receiver, and `GetManialinkPages` on the network types.

## Expected Behavior

The injected methods appear in the member list, like any other member of the
receiver type.

## Actual Behavior

They are absent. The checker ACCEPTS `player.GetCurrentEntityID()` (B059) but
completion will not suggest it, so the tool contradicts itself between two
features: type-check says the member exists, completion says it does not.

## Why

`src/server/completion.rs` `add_type_members` walks the `TypeIndex` (and then
workspace class definitions) only. Openplanet-injected methods are, by
definition, absent from the Nadeo dump — that is the whole premise of B059 —
so a TypeIndex walk can never find them.

This is NOT how the checker finds them: B059 added
`typecheck::builtins::INJECTED_METHODS`, which the checker consults after the
typedb lookups miss. Completion has no equivalent step.

## Work

Wire `INJECTED_METHODS` into `add_type_members` as an additional source, using
the same receiver-matching rule the checker uses (`Checker::injected_method`):
`EveryEngineClass` = derives from CMwNod for the GH #21 pair, exact name match
for the six Mechanism B registrations.

This is WIRING, not discovery: the table already exists and the surface is
bounded, not best-effort. `docs/probes/openplanet-injected-methods.md` proves
the set is closed, so there is no research step.

Two decisions to make while doing it:

- Whether `opCast` / `opImplCast` should be offered. They are injected on
  every engine class, but they back `cast<T>(x)` rather than being written as
  calls, so offering them is probably noise. B059 left them out deliberately.
- Whether hover and signature help need the same source. Hover on
  `GetCurrentEntityID` has nothing to show today, and go-to-definition has no
  source target at all — these methods have no AngelScript declaration
  anywhere, they are registered from C++.

## Test plan

Completion tests live in `src/server/completion.rs`. The existing ones pass
`None` for the `checked` argument; a receiver typed from the typedb needs a
loaded `TypeIndex`, so check whether an existing test already does that before
building new scaffolding.

- `player.` on a `CSmPlayer@` offers `GetCurrentEntityID`
- the same receiver still offers its ordinary dump members (no regression)
- `nod.` on a `CMwNod@` offers `MwAddRef` and `MwRelease`
- each of the four network types offers `GetManialinkPages`
- a receiver OFF the registration list (e.g. `CGameCtnChallenge@`) does NOT
  offer `GetCurrentEntityID` — the same exact-list fence the checker keeps

## Related

B059 (the checker half, and the source table), GH #21.

## Decisions on the two open questions (coordinator, 2026-08-31)

Both questions in the Work section are resolved. Do not re-litigate them.

**1. `opCast` / `opImplCast`: DO NOT offer them. Excluded.**

Verified against the source: `typecheck::builtins::INJECTED_METHODS` holds
exactly four entries — `MwAddRef`, `MwRelease` (EveryEngineClass),
`GetCurrentEntityID` (2 receivers), `GetManialinkPages` (4 receivers). The cast
operators are NOT in it, deliberately, per B059.

The whole defect in this ticket is that the checker and completion contradict
each other about which members exist. Offering `opCast`/`opImplCast` in
completion while the checker does not accept them written as calls would create
a fresh contradiction in the opposite direction — and a worse one, because it
would actively suggest something that then errors. They also back `cast<T>(x)`
syntax rather than being written as method calls, so they would be noise on
every engine-class member list.

**Invariant to hold: completion's injected source and the checker's injected
source are the same table.** If the cast operators are ever added, they are
added to `INJECTED_METHODS` and both features change together. That invariant
is worth a test of its own — assert the set completion offers for a receiver
matches what the checker accepts for it, so the two can never drift again.
That test is more valuable than any individual name assertion here.

**2. Hover / signature help / go-to-definition: split.**

- **Signature help — IN SCOPE** if it resolves members through the same path
  you are wiring. It is the same "does this member exist" question and would
  otherwise be the third feature disagreeing with the other two.
- **Hover — DEFERRED, and not for design reasons.** `src/server/hover.rs` is
  lane-b057's working file for the B056 follow-up, which has an open finding
  that hover does not participate in B052's ancestor walk. Touching hover here
  would collide. File it as a follow-up once that lane lands, and fold it in
  with lane-b057's hover finding rather than as a separate one-line patch.
- **Go-to-definition — NO target exists.** These methods are registered from
  C++ and have no AngelScript declaration anywhere, so there is nothing to jump
  to. Requirement is only that it degrades gracefully: returns no location,
  does not error, does not panic. Add a test that pins the graceful path — an
  unhandled `None` here is the kind of thing that surfaces as a client-side
  crash rather than a missing feature.

**Blocked until lane-b057 lands** (`completion.rs` overlap) and until
lane-b060-b062 lands (`checker.rs` overlap). Branch from master tip after both.

## Lane plan (coordinator, 2026-08-31)

**B063 + B069 + B070 run as ONE lane, serial, in that order**
(`lane-b063-b069-b070`). All three touch `src/server/completion.rs` and/or
`src/server/hover.rs`; run in parallel they would conflict at merge.

**BLOCKED until lane-b057's B056 follow-up merges** (it holds
`fix/b056-followup-canonical-keys`). Branch from that tip, not from now.

**The three are one defect wearing three hats, and that is the framing worth
having before you start.** In every case the CHECKER already has the right
answer and a second feature disagrees with it:

- **B063** — the checker accepts `player.GetCurrentEntityID()` from
  `INJECTED_METHODS`; completion walks only the `TypeIndex` and never offers it.
- **B069** — the resolver rejects `Game::CGameMenu::EMenuOrder` since B056;
  shortcut completion offers it anyway, straight from the loader's storage keys.
- **B070** — the checker accepts `Foo::VirtualKey` via B052's ancestor walk;
  hover asks `lookup_external`, an index-only view that does not know workspace
  namespaces participate, and returns nothing.

So B063 and B070 are features under-reporting what the checker accepts, and
B069 is a feature over-reporting what the checker rejects. **Same root shape:
completion and hover each build their OWN view of what exists instead of
consulting the one the checker already computed.**

Ask explicitly whether one shared accessor — "what does the checker believe is
reachable on this receiver / in this scope?" — closes all three, and answer it
with evidence. Note B060/B061 were given the same framing and the honest answer
there was NO, with structural reasons; that answer was worth as much as a yes.
Do not let the framing widen the tickets: each keeps its own red test and gate.

**One invariant is worth a test regardless of how the fixes are structured:**
what a feature OFFERS and what the checker ACCEPTS must not diverge. That is
the bug in all three, and it is the thing that will regress again.